# Paths that produce collateral merge damage when parallel agents touch them.
#
# Read this before dispatching concurrent work-issue agents. Two pending issues
# whose predicted scopes both intersect a line here are serialized, not
# parallelized. See ~/.claude/skills/work-issue/SKILL.md, "Pre-flight checks".
#
# The cost of getting this wrong is not a merge conflict -- a conflict is loud
# and cheap. It is a plausible-looking resolution that silently breaks an
# invariant nobody re-checks, in a file whose whole job is to be the invariant.

# Every card the engine can run, in one document. Two agents authoring
# different cards both append, and a merge that keeps "both sides" can produce
# a file that still parses and no longer says what either of them meant. It is
# also never re-serialised -- appended textually -- so a tool-assisted merge
# reformats 45,000 lines and buries the change.
datasets/abilities/abilities.json

# Card data and the vendored snapshot behind it. Refreshing either moves counts
# that prose all over the repo restates, so an agent that refreshes invalidates
# the measurements another agent is mid-way through correcting.
datasets/cards/**
datasets/marvelsdb/**

# The rules themselves. A re-harvest renumbers entry ids, and every `[Rule]`
# attribute in the test suite is a reference into it.
datasets/rules-reference/**

# The step vocabulary and the ordering rules built on it. `Steps.Conditions` is
# a table a card can name into: a step added by one agent and a condition added
# by another merge cleanly and describe a window no card reaches.
src/Marvel.Rules/Play/Agenda.cs

# One interpreter, several open issues about different ends of the same
# ordering problem: targets are chosen before payment exists.
src/Marvel.Cards/Run/AbilityRunner.cs
